nd if we were to fit the whole system  naively we could do a much much better  job of it although we still have some  problems so the issue with this is of  course this is this this fundamental  model is not quite working uh and it's  not quite scaling up very well so um  and we can see that because in these  three different configurations we get  very very different parameter values  overall this is showing the percentage  of parameter values that stays the same  across these three circumstances  so so pairwise growth parameters don't  inform a community like a volterra model  very well making this particular model  not particularly good uh in its  formulation for scaling up  yet we all love the lock of altera model  because mentally we can understand what  a species species interaction is  but why is it failing right it's  probably failing because we're missing  um  some kind of interaction so so the lack  of altered description is not sufficient  so what do we do so what we did in our  work was we looked at changing the form  of the equations to account for more  biology in our case because of uh prior  experience with metabolic modeling we  said why don't we try a metabolite  mediated model which is to say that  these microbes are not having direct  species species interactions but they're  interacting through the medium of some  kind of metabolite  and that allows for more complex forms  and and as you can see we can now model  the same thing and we can add  metabolites and  and this becomes more complex and it  also allows us to have more fitting  parameters uh and so it's of course  probably no surprise that that worked  really well  but it also created a really large space  of possibilities  that again relies on human cleverness to  sort of uh curate and understand how to  search appropriately right  otherwise the computer could fit based  on any metabolite it really wanted to  but those might not be the real driver  metabolites  so  so can we  use machine learning  to identify the new functional forms uh  to describe complex systems based on  limited time longitudinal data that's  the question i'm going to pose right now  and and bring forward and show you an  example of  so  the answer is yes uh and i'm going to  show you some work based on a a piece of  the package that's actually remarkably  easy to download and run  that basically  tries to guess what the functional forms  are  and tries to force  those functional forms to be as sparse  as possible that is to have as few terms  as possible  which make it easier to understand from  the human under trying to interpret it  on the other end  so we start with a system of equations  so let me go really slowly here  this is again  on the left hand side of the equation  the rate  of change in x and x can be a species  for instance and there might be many x's  x  1 x 2 x 3.  there is then on the right hand side a  function describing the rate of change  that depends  on the value of  x at a time t  so an example of that  that you all might be familiar with by  now is the sir model that has been  talked about in covet 19 quite a bit  where you have a susceptible population  whose rate in change over time  is given  in proportion to the number of infected  people and the number of susceptible  people they come in contact with and  that's very intuitive because the number  of infected people can go on and infect  more people so the more infections you  have the more rampant it is in the  community  but if there are no susceptible people  it's not going to spread anywhere so  you're not going to grow or lose in this  case because the minus sign any of your  susceptible people  right  so you need a mixture of both  likewise  you have your your infected people and  and they continue to grow according to  the same term because susceptible people  become infected people and uh and then  you know there's some exit rate for this  this system as well  so this is this is an example that you  all might be familiar with just just so  that we understand how this system of  equations works  so uh when we're setting up this problem  what we can do uh using pi cindy is uh  specify a number of functional forms  polynomial forms uh of the different  species x1 x2 so on  and they can be uh  they can be directly proportional they  can be proportional to uh the square uh  the cube and so on and we're going to  set we're going to give it a certain  number of terms to search and then it  also uh can can do these in combination  as well where it's uh you can have terms  where um species one is interacting with  species two species one is interacting  with species one and two and three and  so on in all different combinations with  different powers  up to a certain amount  and uh and  pi cindy says if we can if we can uh  if we if we can make the number of  non-zero terms sparse then we can solve  for the functional form quite well and  very quickly  and as an example of that  we went back to the lock of altera  because everybody likes and for two  species lack of altera we can set up our  own equations set the coefficients and  and look at the the true vector field  and plot it and then we can generate  these five trajectories  well what we can do then is give those  trajectories over to pi cindy and we can  have it impute the vector field over the  whole space and as you can see it does  really really well  just with five trajectories it does a  perfect job essentially of recapturing  a low order equation that describes the  lockable terra vector field  so  that's really good and the question is  could we do it for something more  complex well  if we want to consider a microbiome we  consider all the different absolute  species and species abundances  and we can take this example  uh from  from stephanie's song and and my  collaborator wellesley mark to tell  where they gathered  daily vaginal samples across the course  of 11 weeks or so  and we can take each of these as a  trajectory  and  as a discrete trajectory and then we can  use the same approach to try to  understand the equations of motion  essentially that describe the vector  field  of the microbiome of the vaginal  microbiome  there's the rate of change you'll notice  in the abundance of species x  and i'll just note that this is  approximately the difference between two  subsequent time points  and when we do that and we plot this out  we get something very interesting right  we get a very compelling result which is  that we can generate that vector field  then we can take a random point in the  vector field and we can simulate forward  and we find that we get behavior that's  very similar to what we see in real life  this is the relative abundance of l  inners and blue this is in christmas and  orange and you see here from this  example you have this switching where  this is purple uh this is for spotis is  purple and blue is uh lactobacillus  inners and you can see that they switch  in dominance  very rapidly  and we have that same behavior captured  in our model as well  in addition to that the other thing that  very hap often happens in fact in the  same person just in year 2 of this study  we  we have elk respondus dominance and that  dominance often does not you know it it  fluctuates downward but it really  doesn't go anywhere uh  across time  and and here you know  we have similar behavior although i'll  note there's a numerical instability um  [Music]  and that this is preliminary data that  we're still working through but it's  it's a compelling quantitative result  that we've produced here  so one last thought here  is that  you can think of this  rate of change also as the replication  rate of species x  and for that if you have metagenomics  time longitudinal sampling you actually  can use one of these metagenomic growth  rate estimators there are a few of them  around uh two examples are sort of shown  here um and if you have that you could  perhaps replace this side and actually  improve your estimate even further  i of course am not going to show an  example of that because i don't have  that data but if anybody does feel free  to email me  if if you would like to collaborate and  give that a try  all right  so that's one way in which we can use  machine learning to to improve these  sorts of  uh understanding approaches right  approaches to understanding biology  another one i'd like to  talk about is an evolution and the  particular example we looked at in this  case was cancer evolution but this could  be applied to any population including  microbial populations  and for those you unfamiliar with cancer  one of the paradigms of cancer is that  it starts with a mutation  that mutation grows because it creates a  polarificative advantage  um  more mutations accumulate and so on and  and that's the process of cancer  evolution  until you get to you know a tumor that  that causes health problems  now we can simulate that by considering  genes  in a genome  those genes can have a value of 0 or 1  in our simulation  which is to say 0 is normal and 1 is  mutated  and just for simplicity we can start  with a genome with only  four gene loci  and there are only two driver locis at  the very end at the beginning and end of  that genome  and so that means the cancer genomes are  given by these four genomes over here  that have a one in the first position  and the one in the last position  and the non-cancer genomes are all the  ones that have a zero and one of those  positions are  both and so we can simulate this forward  now  that is we can simulate the evolution of  cancer we can mark down  the progression of that cancer and then  we can use those  um to try to understand  what's happening now how are we going to  use those  well we can use a technique called  inverse reinforcement learning and i  don't want to dive too deep into this  but inverse reinforcement learning  basically takes a set of expert  demonstrations  which is to say  demonstrations of cancer evolution and  it  infers a reward function  that describes  the incentives and preferences that that  agent is going to behave with  that is to say it's understanding how  cancer behaves it's forming the reward  map of cancer in this case and from our  example i already told you that the  first and last because we put this in we  knew what the answer was i told you that  the first and last position  were the positions that were driver  mutations and what you can see in this  simulation  is that indeed that's true  the first position in the last position  if you have if they're both zero are  approximately equally rewarded in this  reward map  in terms of these actions which are  mutating the first or the last position  if the first position is already mutated  the last position is what really wants  to be picked up  if it's the opposite the last position  is mutated then the first position is  the one that's the most rewarding and so  you can see that this reward function  describes the behaviors  that would drive cancer and so this  becomes a really interesting paradigm  for understanding cancer evolution  and the question we ask  is can we learn what incentivizes tumor  growth  can we take  the behavior of cancer that is the  expert demonstrations that come from  cancer  which we're going to estimate using  tumor phylogeny  and recover meaningful reward functions  that describe  tumor growth  so for this we created an algorithm  which we called the pop-up restaurant  for uh inverse reinforcement learning or  pure irl  the reason we call it the pop-up  restaurant process is because  it involves essentially a lot of chinese  restaurant processes for partitioning  expert demonstrations into different  reward functions  and because the name matches quite well  a pop-up restaurant is a short-term  restaurant that allows chefs to try out  new concepts new audiences and  experiment with new ingredients and  that's pretty much what we're doing  we're trying new reward functions  reshuffling the expert demonstrations  into different restaurants and and  trying uh different types of data in all  of these until we find sort of the best  result  and again not going to go into the  details of this  but you know truth in advertising this  is the pure irl implementation and  anyone who wants to read this is welcome  to go through our paper which uh i'll  i'll show at the end of the talk  so the result of this  is that  we showed that the two most  rewarded paths the two most likely paths  that occur after learning the reward  functions  are these two shown here this is sort of  the reward this is the reward function  up here although it's really hard to  read because you can see this space is  really really large  what i can say about this space is that  they're about 10 to the 200 possible  places you can go here  or different orders in which you can do  things  and the top two give us these uh  orderings abc k ras maybe smab 4 tp53  leading to cancer and what's really  interesting is this exactly matches the  second path exactly matches  the 1990 seminal paper by fearson and  vogelstein which described the  progression of colorectal cancer that is  still considered canonical today  so in other words we managed to  rediscover this path uh and we only used  data from 27 patients with no time  longitudinal data just inferring  based on tumor phylogeny which gives us  only a very approximate sense of time  but this really shows us the power of  this method and it has the potential to  add it doesn't just rediscover what  humans already know it has the potential  to discover new things  and so some of these new things we  looked at some of the more likely  transitions from normal that were  considered drivers in this model  and  we found these papers for all three of  these we instantly found papers that  indicated either experimental evidence  or epidemiological evidence from meta  studies  that showed these are  early drivers  in colorectal cancer  so this is really a new paradigm for us  pushing forward  to use the computer to augment  biological understanding  so in conclusion i want us all to  understand that biological systems are  really complex and have complex behavior  machine learning is a way in which we  can embrace that  and especially exciting  are the growth avenues uh that involve  understanding the infinite dynamics such  as those infinite dynamics  that include um you know evolution  or microbiome behavior ecological  behavior and those are the areas that  are really exciting and upcoming in the  future for the microbiome fields here  are a bunch of papers for future reading  and i want to give thanks to everyone in  the lab for this and my collaborators my  funding sources i'll uh highlight a few  people in particular here john  collentari  who now runs his own lab and group  within mayo clinic  was the post-doc who who did the work on  inverse reinforcement learning jiren  berner did the work on the ecological  models and the metabolite mediated  models and is now moving on to uh  postdoc in los alamos national lab  stephanie song who i mentioned earlier  provided the vaginal microbiome data  for us to work with  and  yash law actually did the work uh with  the vector fields  that i showed in the middle of this talk  so thank you very much  and with that  i hope you all had a good time
 

